[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1714","stub":{"id":"1714","section":"Base of Upper Falls to Lake Jocassee","altname":"","river":"Whitewater","difficulty":"IItoVplus","states":["USA-SCR"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":null,"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"111865","location":{"latitude":"35.0360984802246","longitude":"-83.0182037353516"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"117137","location":{"latitude":"35.0049018859863","longitude":"-82.9922027587891"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"2.5","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"4246","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4856","description":"Shot taken day after rivers hit their peek in the SE","caption":"Whitewater Falls","poi_name":"Whitewater Falls","subject":"","author":"Mark Hammock","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/4246","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/4246"}},{"id":"18759","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5460","description":"","caption":"entering the top teacups","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/18759","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/18759"}},{"id":"18766","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5460","description":"","caption":"don't miss the eddy","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/18766","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/18766"}},{"id":"18760","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5460","description":"","caption":"sitting below the second teacup","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/18760","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/18760"}},{"id":"18761","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5460","description":"","caption":"tony on the bigger one","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/18761","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/18761"}},{"id":"18762","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5460","description":"","caption":"Kirk on the bigger one","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/18762","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/18762"}},{"id":"18763","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5460","description":"","caption":"lower slide","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/18763","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/18763"}},{"id":"18764","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5460","description":"","caption":"blasting the bottom of the lower slide","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/18764","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/18764"}},{"id":"18765","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5460","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/18765","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/18765"}}],"detail":{"id":"1714","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"Half of this run is unrunnable and half is flatwater. There are two very short stretches of interest. The general progression of the run is as follows:\n\n400 foot waterfall\n\nQuarter mile of steep huge boulder gardens that make the Upper West Prong look small\n\nMile and a half of flatwater\n\n100 yards of fun low water hike and huck right up to the lip of...\n\nLower Whitewater Falls - 200 feet\n\nOne long toxawayesque slide that MIGHT be runnable, a few jumbles, then...\n\nMoondance Falls, unrunnable series of cascades totalling over 100 feet.\n\nOne class 5 boulder jumble right into Lake Jocassee.\n\nI will note that the lower gorge from below Lower Whitewater Falls to the lake is incredibly walled in and should be scouted thoroughly before committing. There is not an absolute way in yet and portaging Moondance Falls may or may NOT be possible. Go in real low to scope it, reallizing you will likely be doing it all for one narrow slide and a boof or two. 90% of this gorge is likely to be portaged.\n\nThe hike and huck stretch immediately above the gorge is fun and not super difficult, but going in there with any water or recent rains is a BAD idea. The last drop ends in a 10 foot long pool immediately above Lower Whitewater Falls, which is a 60 foot entrance slide off 100 feet of vertical reconnecting on a shelf with several more bone crushing claps over 80 more vertical feet, before landing on a big pile of rocks. Instadeath!\n\nUse your brain and don't get too greedy for a view of the falls, the rocks are slick and this is a true no mistake environment if there is one in the SE. That said, if you are tired of Triple Falls and there aint no water, this can be a rewarding afternoon well spent.\n\nThe drops progress as follows:\n\nTeacups: A [sliding little teacup](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/Photo/detail/photoid/18759/) into a nice little [3 foot boof](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/Photo/detail/photoid/18760/) teacup, right above the big one. No brainers.\n\nHot and ready: A nice off vertical [28 foot falls](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/Photo/detail/photoid/18761/) with piton potential for the off-line boater. Head center [right off the middle of the kicker](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/Photo/detail/photoid/18762/) and keep your weight forward, as you are likely to reconnect right when hitting the bottom. Fast and fun. Spots to avoid are far right and far left.\n\nCurtain Call: A fun slide above the lip of Lower Whitewater Falls. Two lines, one is [far right](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/Photo/detail/photoid/18763/), the fastest, but center there is a tech line to try for the pothole boof of about 8 feet. Either way, [don't miss the eddy](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/Photo/detail/photoid/18766/) for god sakes.\n\nThe other whitewater stretch is the breakdown of Upper Whitewater Falls. This quarter mile stretch drops around 600 feet/mile and is super sick. Maybe a slightly cleaner Garden of the Gods if you will. The boofs are 12 footers, with class 5 approaches and washouts. Not many eddies. Big hike in for little return. Why weren't you on Thompson or the normal Whitewater Run might be a good question at the takeout at Bad Creek. But to each his own.\n\nLogistics: To run the gnar-gnar to flatwater upper part, hike down the foothills trail from 281 on river right through the falls overlook area down to the base, then bushwack up to the top of the rapids, take out at the musterground bridge or foothills trail bridge, hike 1/2 mile back to Bad Creek parking area.\n\nLogistics: To do the dry days hike and huck, park at Bad Creek access, hike 1/2 mile down musterground road, put on at the bridge, careful to dial the level as bone dry before blinding the goods around the corner. Below here who knows. Do your homework!","averageGradient":"440","maxGradient":"850","length":"3","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["111865","117137"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-83.01802780848928,35.03623909875013],[-83.01794898157982,35.036140478950244],[-83.01766191491362,35.03594241228393],[-83.01755491491377,35.0358